  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Frost/freeze conditions are possible tonight through \nSunday night.\n\nA high pressure system of 1025 mb will continue to shift southward \nover the Mid-west through daybreak. This will yield steady H85 CAA \nthrough this early morning, therefore expect low temperatures in the \nmorning to drop into the mid 30s across inland counties with low to \nmid 40s across the coastal counties. Also, calm to light \nnorthwesterly winds around 5 mph will preserve through daybreak. \nThus, the combination of the low temperatures, the light to calm \nwinds, and RH values ranging in the mid to upper 90s will result in \nareas to patchy frost along and west of the I-95 corridor. A Frost \nAdvisory has been issued for this area from 2AM to 8AM this morning \nas a result.\n\nContinuing on through the rest of today, the high pressure will \nexpand into the mid and upper MS Valley, with it's periphery \nstretching into the Southeastern CONUS. Expect northerly winds to \ncontinue to usher cold air into the region throughout tonight. \nDespite an ample amount of sunshine, afternoon highs will only reach \ninto the upper 50s to low 60s. The combination of clear skies with \ncalm to light winds will lead to a good amount of radiational \ncooling. As a result, expect overnight lows to drop into upper 20s \nto low 30s across the inland counties and mid 30s to low 40s across \nthe coastal counties. Therefore, there was a good amount of \nconfidence to upgrade the Freeze Watch to a Freeze Warning for early \nSunday morning across the interior southeast Georgia and southeast \nSouth Carolina counties. The forecast notes widespread to areas of \nfrost to account for this. It's possible we might need to issue a \nFrost Advisory with the next forecast update for a couple counties \neast of the Freeze Warning.\n\nIf widespread freezing temperatures occur on Saturday night into \nSunday morning, then this will end the local growing season and \nFrost/Freeze products will not be issued for Sunday night into \nMonday morning. However, if only some areas observe freezing \ntemperatures, then the growing season continues and we will likely \nneed Frost/Freeze products on Sunday night into Monday morning. \nRegardless of the outcome, overnight temperatures will be below \nnormal for this time of year.",
